  "slug": "development-reveals-5600",
  "summary": "Single-cell and cross-species multiomics now resolve human-specific regulatory elements that govern cortical progenitor states.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the regulatory logic underlying human-enriched features of cortical development. Locus-resolved long-read profiling in cortical organoids links human-specific transposable elements to the timing of the radial glia-to-neuron transition, tested by CRISPRi manipulation. Cross-species multiomics across human, macaque and mouse fetal cortex identifies RHOJ as an oRG-enriched regulator of human-enriched progenitor states. A single-nucleus accessibility atlas of 1.17M candidate enhancers across 104k mid-gestation human cortical nuclei provides a lineage-restricted regulatory reference. Two further preprints address non-cortical loci: a human-accelerated enhancer contacts FOXC1 and human-specific substitutions boost chondrocyte activity, and parent-of-origin chromatin conformation at Dlk1-Dio3 depends on the germline imprinting control region and can be decoupled from expression. The new element is the combination of locus-resolved perturbation with cross-species and single-nucleus resolution.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
